{"explanation":"The geometric upper bound for the dimension error tends to zero because $CB<1$ and $C\\theta/B>1$. The collision expression $\\eta^2(B/A)^m/(2(m+1))$ tends to infinity because $B/A>1$ and $\\eta>0$. Eventually $C^m\\geq2$ and $m\\geq1$ as well. Intersect these four eventual conditions and choose one common index $m$. The floor bound for $\\lfloor C^m\\rfloor$ transfers the upper bound to the actual dimension error, giving the first strict inequality; the collision limit gives the second. Source: OpenAI math family 017, commit adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a.","natural_language_statement":"Let $\\theta,A,B,C,\\eta,a,b,\\varepsilon,T\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $\\theta>0$, $B>0$, $C>1$, $CB<1$, $C\\theta/B>1$, $B/A>1$, $\\eta>0$, $a,b\\geq0$, and $\\varepsilon>0$. There exists an integer $m\\geq1$ for which\n\n$$\\frac{am+b}{V_m}+100\\frac{K_m}{W_m}<\\varepsilon,\\qquad T<\\frac{\\eta^2(B/A)^m}{2(m+1)}.$$\n\nHere $K_m=\\lfloor C^m\\rfloor$, $W_m=(B^m)^{-1}$, and $V_m=2K_m\\theta^mW_m$. This common large index balances a decaying dimension error with a growing collision margin.","source_witness":{"source_commit":"adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a","source_components":[{"facts":"missions/pi-irrationality-measure/verification/sketch-DimensionLimits-20261007-223436.log","facts_sha256":"c431defdda2a5966dfcc5ea6b7fd5014b5253273866aab3a0ca929fc712762be","original_source":"missions/pi-irrationality-measure/source/lean/OAI/NumberTheory/PiExponent/Analysis/DimensionLimits.lean","original_source_sha256":"57bd77d4eeaa3bcbf7d18a52124adc24e5339c87037bc90154c1cb5de177b9cf","source":"missions/pi-irrationality-measure/port/lean/OAI/NumberTheory/PiExponent/Analysis/DimensionLimits.lean","source_module":"OAI.NumberTheory.PiExponent.Analysis.DimensionLimits","source_sha256":"25abcb10165890a64fce08bf1f36492cd4b4f5031dc206d1f1b841f1baa7d66f"},{"facts":"missions/pi-irrationality-measure/verification/sketch-Dimension-20261008-030107.log","facts_sha256":"92e7217859